{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/concurrencyandcancellation"]}],"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","title":"Concurrency & Cancellation","role":"article"},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ConcurrencyAndCancellation","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"text":"The guarantees ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" makes about overlapping, cancelled, and reset calls.","type":"text"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Related Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"],"anchor":"Related-Documentation"},{"title":"Modeling state","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePreviewError","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Pagination"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Modeling-state"}],"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"code":"@MainActor","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and built under Swift 6 strict concurrency. Every "},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"refreshCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadWithRetry","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loadNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" call is "},{"code":"async","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", which raises three questions the moment a screen does more than one of them: what happens if a second call starts before the first finishes? How do you stop one early? And how do you force a store back to a known state outside of any of them? This article covers all three."}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overlapping calls only ever apply the latest result","anchor":"Overlapping-calls-only-ever-apply-the-latest-result"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Nothing extra is needed to handle a screen firing off a second call before an earlier one has finished — a fast double-tap on Retry, or a new search query arriving before the previous one resolved:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 1) } \/\/ slow — still in flight","await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 2) } \/\/ fast — resolves first"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Internally, every call stamps itself with a token before running ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and only applies its result — ","type":"text"},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", or the ","type":"text"},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" mapping ","type":"text"},{"code":"loadCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" do — if that token is still the most recently started one by the time ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" resolves. In the example above, article 1’s slower fetch resolving ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"after"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" article 2’s result is already showing never overwrites it; its result is silently dropped. ","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" follows the same rule: it only clears once the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"newest","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" in-flight refresh finishes, not whichever overlapping one happens to resolve first. ","type":"text"},{"code":"loadNextPage(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"isLoadingNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" follows it too.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You don’t need to call ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","isActive":true},{"text":" for this to hold — it’s automatic. Reach for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cancel()"},{"text":" when you additionally want to stop the older call’s work from running at all, not just prevent its result from landing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Cancelling-explicitly","level":2,"text":"Cancelling explicitly"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Call "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" to stop whatever call is currently running, without waiting for it to resolve:"}]},{"code":["store.cancel() \/\/ e.g. from onDisappear, or right before firing off a newer query"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","